      { term: 'Emergency Fund', definition: 'Cash reserves dedicated exclusively to unexpected, necessary expenses, medical bills, car repairs, job loss, or urgent home repairs. Not for planned expenses or wants.', example: 'A $10,000 emergency fund in a HYSA earning 4.5% gives you $450/year in interest while protecting you from financial shocks.', sentence: 'Her emergency fund covered the $2,200 ER bill in cash, so she never had to touch a credit card or go into debt.' },
      { term: 'High-Yield Savings Account (HYSA)', definition: 'An FDIC-insured savings account, typically from an online bank, paying 4-5% APY, roughly 50-100x more than traditional bank savings rates.', example: 'Ally Bank, Marcus by Goldman Sachs, and Capital One 360 offer HYSAs with no minimum balance and no fees.', sentence: 'Moving her emergency fund from Chase (0.01% APY) to a HYSA (4.50% APY) earned her an extra $449 per year on $10,000, for doing nothing.' },
      { term: 'FDIC Insurance', definition: 'Federal government guarantee that protects bank deposits up to $250,000 per depositor per institution. Covers both traditional and online banks equally.', example: 'If your HYSA bank fails, the FDIC returns every dollar up to $250,000, typically within 2 business days.', sentence: 'FDIC insurance means your emergency fund in an online bank is exactly as safe as money in a traditional big bank, the government backs both.' },
      { term: 'APY (Annual Percentage Yield)', definition: 'The total amount of interest earned on a deposit over one year, accounting for compound interest. The true measure of savings account returns.', example: '4.50% APY on $5,000 = $225 in interest earned over one year.', sentence: 'Always compare savings accounts by APY, not APR, APY includes the compounding effect and shows what you actually earn.' },
      { term: 'Financial Fragility', definition: 'A state where a person or household cannot absorb a financial shock without going into debt, missing payments, or experiencing severe hardship.', example: 'The Federal Reserve found 37% of Americans are financially fragile, unable to cover a $400 expense without borrowing.', sentence: 'Financial fragility affects people at all income levels, 30% of those earning over $100K also live paycheck to paycheck.' },
      { term: 'Sinking Fund', definition: 'A separate savings fund for planned, expected expenses, car maintenance, holiday gifts, annual insurance premiums, so they don\'t become "emergencies."', example: 'Setting aside $50/month for car maintenance means a $600 repair is planned spending, not an emergency.', sentence: 'Building sinking funds for predictable expenses reduces the drain on your emergency fund and keeps it reserved for true surprises.' }
